Barnaby was not your average house cat.

He was a ginger tabby with a white chest, a torn left ear, and eyes the color of old pennies. While other cats in the neighborhood were content to nap on windowsills, chase dust motes, and demand breakfast at precisely the wrong hour, Barnaby spent most of his time watching the world beyond the glass. He watched the wind move through the maple leaves. He watched the mail carrier walk down the street. He watched the foxes slip between the hedges at dusk, their tails low and their eyes bright with secrets.

His humans called him “the explorer,” though they said it mostly when he knocked things off the counter.

“You’re too curious for your own good,” his human, Mara, would say, picking up a pen he had batted onto the floor.

Barnaby would blink slowly, as if to say, *The world is full of things worth investigating.*

One crisp autumn evening, the back door was left open just a crack.

It was only a small gap, barely wide enough for a cat to squeeze through, but to Barnaby it might as well have been a grand gate thrown open to the universe. The kitchen was warm behind him, the food bowl half full, the couch soft and familiar. But the night air carried scents he could not ignore: damp earth, fallen leaves, the distant sweetness of rain, and something else—something sharp and strange that made his whiskers twitch.

He slipped through the door without a sound.

The garden looked different at night. In daylight, it was a tidy little rectangle of grass, flower beds, and a wooden fence. At night, it became a kingdom of shadows. The rose bushes stood like thorny sentinels. The garden hose lay curled in the grass like a sleeping snake. The old oak tree at the far end of the yard rose above everything else, its branches black against the moon.

Barnaby had never gone beyond the garden before.

He paused at the edge of the grass, one paw raised. His tail flicked. Every instinct told him to turn back. The house was warm. Mara was inside. There were treats in the cupboard and a soft blanket on the couch. But the scent in the air pulled him forward, and he stepped into the night.

The grass was cool beneath his paws. He moved carefully, low to the ground, the way his mother had taught him before she disappeared one winter and never returned. He had learned young that the world was not always kind. It was beautiful, yes, but it was also full of things with teeth.

He crossed the garden and reached the fence.

The wooden boards were old and warped, with a gap near the bottom where the ground had sunk. Barnaby squeezed through, his fur brushing against the damp wood. On the other side was the alley behind the houses, a narrow strip of cracked pavement, weeds, and trash cans. It smelled of rain, garbage, and something metallic.

He stepped into the alley and felt very small.

The moon hung low between the rooftops, pale and round. A streetlight buzzed at the far end, casting a sickly yellow glow over the pavement. Somewhere above him, a crow called out, its voice rough and strange. Barnaby’s ears flattened. He had heard crows before, but never so close.

He moved forward, keeping to the shadows.

The alley led to a small park he had seen from the car window many times but never explored. There was a rusted swing set, a broken bench, and a cluster of trees whose branches tangled together like fingers. Beyond them, the street opened into a wider road lined with shops. Most were closed for the night, their windows dark, their signs dim.

Barnaby had never been this far from home.

A thrill ran through him, sharp and bright. He was an explorer now. He was a hunter. He was something wild.

He padded across the pavement, his claws
